<h1>How to Get More MSP Clients Without Relying on Referrals</h1>
<p class="lf-lede">A practical MSP client-acquisition process starts with a clear ideal customer profile, a compelling reason to change IT providers, relevant marketing channels and consistent sales follow-up.</p>
</div></header>
<div class="container lf-article-body">
<p class="mp-article-byline">By LeadsFlo Editorial · November 3, 2026</p>
<div class="mp-article-answer"><strong>THE SHORT ANSWER</strong><p>A practical MSP client-acquisition process starts with a clear ideal customer profile, a compelling reason to change IT providers, relevant marketing channels and consistent sales follow-up.</p></div>
<div class="mp-article-cta"><strong>MSP MARKETING & LEAD GENERATION</strong><p>Connect relevant demand, qualification, sales follow-up and appointment setting.</p><a href="/msp-lead-generation/">Explore MSP Lead Generation ↗</a></div>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Define the businesses you actually want</h2><p>Start with your service capacity, geography, preferred industries and minimum viable agreement size. A 12-user business looking for occasional laptop repairs requires a very different model from a 150-user organization evaluating fully managed IT.</p><p>Specify the right company size, number of locations, technical environment and recurring-service fit. Then use those criteria to evaluate inquiries instead of reporting every form submission as a qualified lead.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Give prospects a reason to start a conversation</h2><p>Most businesses already have some arrangement for IT support. Explain what would make a conversation worthwhile: inconsistent service, compliance requirements, a contract review, rapid growth or a need for more strategic IT planning.</p><p>Avoid relying on vague promises about being proactive or providing excellent service. Give concrete examples of the discovery process, supported services, onboarding and what a prospective customer can expect.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Build inbound demand around commercial intent</h2><p>Use service and location pages to explain managed IT, co-managed support, cloud services and cybersecurity offerings. Search campaigns can capture active buying demand while educational content helps prospects understand whether your MSP is suitable.</p><p>Your website needs an obvious next step: a discovery call, an appropriately scoped assessment or a request for a conversation. Track those actions separately from casual page visits.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Use outbound without turning it into spam</h2><p>Relevant outbound prospecting can complement inbound marketing. Research businesses that match your target profile, personalize the reason for reaching out and respect applicable email, privacy and calling requirements.</p><p>Outbound should create a legitimate business conversation. Sending larger volumes of generic emails is not a substitute for researching fit and offering a meaningful reason to respond.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Treat follow-up as part of acquisition</h2><p>Record who owns an inquiry, when the first response occurred, what the prospect needs and the next action. Build reminders around real sales milestones rather than a fixed sequence of messages disconnected from the conversation.</p><p>An MSP prospect may be months away from a contract review. Keep the buying window visible and follow up appropriately rather than marking the opportunity lost after an unanswered call.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Measure new clients, not just leads</h2><p>Track qualified inquiries, meetings booked, meetings attended, proposals, closed-won agreements and new monthly recurring revenue. Calculate acquisition cost with both advertising spend and sales or delivery-related costs considered.</p><p>Review which channels produce suitable accounts. A campaign with fewer but better-qualified conversations may be more valuable than one with a low cost per unqualified lead.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What is the fastest way to get more MSP clients?</h3><p>There is no universal fastest channel. Start by improving follow-up on existing demand, clarifying your ideal customer profile and testing one high-intent acquisition channel.</p>
<h3>Should MSPs stop relying on referrals?</h3><p>No. Referrals can remain valuable, but a documented acquisition process helps reduce dependence on unpredictable introductions.</p></section>
